## Why This Is Asked

Testing at scale with multi-tenant isolation and controlled chaos reveals critical gaps in data separation, retry policies, observability, and disaster recovery mechanisms. This question assesses a candidate's understanding of end-to-end reliability, fault tolerance, and security in distributed systems.

## Key Concepts

- **End-to-end testing at scale**: Validating system behavior under realistic concurrent load
- **Multi-tenant data isolation**: Ensuring tenants cannot access or view each other's data
- **Fault injection and chaos engineering**: Deliberately introducing failures to test system resilience
- **Auditability and data integrity**: Verifying that orders remain consistent and all actions are logged
- **Time-bounded verification**: Confirming the system recovers within acceptable SLA windows

## Code Example

```javascript
// k6 load test with fault injection
import http from 'k6/http';
import { check, sleep } from 'k6';

export const options = {
  vus: 1000,
  duration: '30s',
  thresholds: {
    http_req_duration: ['p(95)<500'],
  },
};

const tenants = ['tenant-a', 'tenant-b', 'tenant-c'];
const coupons = { 'tenant-a': 'DISC10', 'tenant-b': 'SAVE20', 'tenant-c': 'FREESHIP' };

export default function () {
  const tenant = tenants[Math.floor(Math.random() * tenants.length)];
  const coupon = coupons[tenant];

  // Apply coupon to cart
  const cartRes = http.post('https://gateway/api/cart', 
    JSON.stringify({ tenant, productId: 'prod-123', coupon }),
    { headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' } }
  );

  check(cartRes, {
    'tenant isolation': (r) => r.json('tenant') === tenant,
    'discount applied': (r) => r.json('discount') > 0,
    'status is 200': (r) => r.status === 200,
  });

  // Place order
  const orderRes = http.post('https://gateway/api/orders',
    JSON.stringify({ tenant, cartId: cartRes.json('cartId') }),
    { headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' } }
  );

  check(orderRes, {
    'order created': (r) => r.status === 201,
    'order integrity': (r) => r.json('orderId') !== undefined,
  });

  sleep(1);
}

// Fault injection via Kubernetes API:
// 1. Redis cache miss: kubectl exec redis-0 -- redis-cli FLUSHDB
// 2. DB failover: kubectl delete pod postgres-primary-0
```

## Why This Question Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to design reproducible, scalable end-to-end tests that validate strict data isolation in a real-time, tenant-separated system. It also tests understanding of failure scenarios that expose flaky caching or routing logic.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end test orchestration across ingestion, broker, cache, storage, and UI
- Per-tenant data isolation and security boundaries
- Real-time WebSocket delivery and latency budgets
- Fault injection and resilience under peak load
- Telemetry, tracing, and reproducibility

## Code Example

```javascript
// Seed data and tenant configuration
const tenants = [
  { id: 'tenant-a', name: 'Acme Corp', apiKey: 'key_a_xxx' },
  { id: 'tenant-b', name: 'Beta Inc', apiKey: 'key_b_xxx' },
  { id: 'tenant-c', name: 'Gamma LLC', apiKey: 'key_c_xxx' },
  { id: 'tenant-d', name: 'Delta Co', apiKey: 'key_d_xxx' },
  { id: 'tenant-e', name: 'Epsilon Ltd', apiKey: 'key_e_xxx' },
];

// Traffic pattern: 20k events/second per tenant
const EVENTS_PER_SECOND = 20000;
const DURATION_SECONDS = 60;

async function ingestEvents(tenant, emitFn) {
  const interval = 1000 / EVENTS_PER_SECOND;
  for (let i = 0; i < DURATION_SECONDS * EVENTS_PER_SECOND; i++) {
    emitFn({
      tenantId: tenant.id,
      eventId: uuid(),
      timestamp: Date.now(),
      payload: { value: Math.random(), tag: tenant.id },
    });
    await sleep(interval);
  }
}

// Fault injection: broker drop
async function injectBrokerDrop(broker, dropPercent = 5) {
  const originalPublish = broker.publish.bind(broker);
  broker.publish = async (topic, msg) => {
    if (Math.random() * 100 < dropPercent) return; // drop
    return originalPublish(topic, msg);
  };
}

// Fault injection: cache eviction
function evictCacheKey(cache, keyPattern) {
  const keys = cache.keys().filter(k => keyPattern.test(k));
  keys.forEach(k => cache.delete(k));
}

// Verification: detect cross-tenant leakage
async function verifyIsolation(dashboard, expectedTenantId) {
  const messages = await dashboard.getReceivedMessages();
  for (const msg of messages) {
    if (msg.tenantId !== expectedTenantId) {
      throw new Error(`CROSS-TENANT LEAK: ${msg.tenantId} seen on ${expectedTenantId}'s dashboard`);
    }
  }
}

// Latency verification
function verifyLatency(msgTimestamp, receiveTimestamp) {
  const latency = receiveTimestamp - msgTimestamp;
  if (latency > 2000) {
    throw new Error(`LATENCY BREACH: ${latency}ms exceeds 2s threshold`);
  }
  return latency;
}

// Main test orchestration
async function runE2ETest() {
  const broker = new MessageBroker();
  const cache = new TenantCache();
  const storage = new TenantStorage();
  const dashboards = {};

  // Setup WebSocket dashboards per tenant
  for (const tenant of tenants) {
    dashboards[tenant.id] = new WebSocketDashboard({
      authToken: tenant.apiKey,
      onMessage: (msg, ts) => {
        verifyIsolation(dashboards[tenant.id], tenant.id);
        verifyLatency(msg.timestamp, ts);
      },
    });
  }

  // Inject faults mid-test
  setTimeout(() => injectBrokerDrop(broker, 5), 30000);
  setTimeout(() => evictCacheKey(cache, /^tenant-a/), 45000);

  // Run traffic for all tenants in parallel
  await Promise.all(
    tenants.map(tenant => ingestEvents(tenant, msg => broker.publish(`${tenant.id}/events`, msg)))
  );

  // Final assertions
  console.log('All events processed. Verifying final state...');
  for (const tenant of tenants) {
    const storageData = await storage.getAll(tenant.id);
    assert.strictEqual(storageData.length, EVENTS_PER_SECOND * DURATION_SECONDS);
    console.log(`✓ ${tenant.name}: ${storageData.length} events stored`);
  }
}
```

## What to Look For in a Strong Answer
1. Clear test structure covering setup, execution, and assertions
2. Proper isolation verification at each layer (cache, broker, storage)
3. Realistic fault injection with measurable impact
4. Latency assertions tied to actual message timestamps
5. Reproducibility through seed data and deterministic traffic

## Why This Is Asked
Tests for offline-first applications with service workers and cross-tab interactions are common in modern web development. This question evaluates practical E2E thinking around offline caching, network replay, idempotent API design, and conflict resolution at a beginner level.

## Key Concepts
- Offline-first UX and service workers
- Local storage and IndexedDB persistence
- Idempotent server updates with PUT
- Cross-tab concurrency and conflict handling
- Deterministic data seeds for repeatable tests

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright E2E test for offline-first note app
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test.describe('Offline-first note app', () => {
  test('should create note offline, sync when online, and handle idempotency', async ({ page }) => {
    // Set up deterministic seed for reproducible data
    const noteId = 'test-note-001';
    const noteContent = 'Test note content';

    // Simulate offline by intercepting network requests
    await page.route('**/api/**', (route) => route.abort('failed'));

    // Create note while offline
    await page.goto('/app');
    await page.fill('[data-testid="note-editor"]', noteContent);
    await page.click('[data-testid="save-button"]');

    // Verify note is cached locally
    const localNote = await page.evaluate(() => localStorage.getItem('draft'));
    expect(localNote).toContain(noteContent);

    // Restore network and mock PUT endpoint
    await page.unroute('**/api/**');
    await page.route('**/api/notes/**', (route) => {
      route.fulfill({ status: 200, body: JSON.stringify({ revision: 'rev-123' }) });
    });

    // Trigger sync
    await page.click('[data-testid="sync-button"]');
    await expect(page.locator('[data-testid="sync-status"]')).toHaveText('Synced');

    // Verify idempotent behavior - re-submit should not create duplicate
    const initialRequestCount = 1;
    await page.click('[data-testid="sync-button"]');
    // Assert no duplicate entries on server
  });
});
```

